# Continuous Training Pipeline — Plan (Revised)

## Goal

Replace the phased iterate/train/eval/reject loop with a continuous,
KataGo-style pipeline. Selfplay, training, gatekeeper, and (optional)
rating run concurrently as independent processes, coordinating only via
the filesystem.

Scope for this plan: **single-host, single selfplay writer**. The
selfplay driver is the sole owner of filename allocation, staging
cleanup, and disk pruning (see Selfplay driver section). Scaling
selfplay to multiple hosts or processes is NOT covered here — doing
that safely requires a per-writer ID namespace (e.g.
`g_w{worker_id:02d}_{seq:017d}.bin.zst`) and a coordinated pruner, and
those changes ripple into the scanner watermark format and the crash-
recovery protocol. When we need cluster-scale, design that as a
follow-up; do not assume this plan covers it.

Training DDP scaling (multiple GPUs on ONE host) is covered — see the
DDP ownership section.

Fresh start only. No warm-start from any prior run.

Target hardware for default knobs: 2×4090, ResNet 64f/5b.

Peak selfplay-only throughput on this hardware is ~3-10 games/s (0.1-0.3
s/game). But in continuous mode all four workers share the same GPUs —
training is running during selfplay, the gatekeeper briefly takes over
for matches, and occasional TRT engine rebuilds pause everything. Under
that contention, **sustained selfplay is ~1-2 games/s**, not the peak.
The trainer is further throttled by the replay-ratio bucket (only fills
as fast as new selfplay rows arrive), so **sustained training is ~0.3-0.6
steps/s**, far below the ~5-10 steps/s the GPUs can push in isolation.

Default knobs below are sized conservatively against those sustained
rates: a conservative 1-week run yields roughly **150-250k training
steps** and **25-50 candidates offered**, hitting at least the first LR
milestone and producing a measurable Elo curve. If actual throughput
comes in higher, the run reaches further into the LR schedule — no
re-tuning needed. If lower, the run still completes meaningful work.

We are explicitly **not** aiming for a full converged model on this first
run. The target is: prove the pipeline end-to-end, observe strength gain
on the Elo chart, and collect signal about whether the throughput
assumptions hold. If it works, scale up (more GPUs, larger model, longer
run) with confidence.


## Source-backed alignment with KataGo

Design mirrors KataGo's public training code (`github.com/lightvector/KataGo`).
Where we deviate it is for small-scale simplification, noted inline.

- Gatekeeper: binary accept/reject by raw winrate ≥ 0.5, default 200 games
  at 150 visits. `cpp/command/gatekeeper.cpp:271,581`,
  `cpp/configs/training/gatekeeper1.cfg`. Go-specific deviation: we count
  only wins (draws count as losses for the candidate) — draws are
  essentially impossible under area scoring + half-integer komi, and
  counting draws as losses is stricter.
- Replay ratio via train-bucket: `-max-train-bucket-per-new-data = 4` in
  KataGo. Each new selfplay row adds `replay_target` samples of training
  budget; trainer drains the bucket one sample at a time; stalls when
  empty. `python/train.py:102`, `SelfplayTraining.md`.
- Export: step-count-based via `-epochs-per-export`. Filenames encode step
  count and total rows consumed. `python/train.py:87, 2628-2630`.
- Promotion mechanism: filesystem `rename()` into `--accepted-models-dir`.
  KataGo's selfplay polls that dir every 20s in C++. We poll in Python
  between `build/selfplay` invocations instead (no C++ changes).
  `cpp/command/gatekeeper.cpp:225-240`, `cpp/command/selfplay.cpp:332-347`.
- Adam state: KataGo persists optimizer state across restarts in their
  ckpt.ckpt. We do too — cold-starting Adam on every restart throws away
  useful momentum and there are no phase boundaries in continuous training
  that justify discarding it.
- Rating/match: a separate `match` subcommand for tournament Elo across
  many nets. Not tied to promotion. `cpp/command/match.cpp:39`.


## Motivation

The current phased loop has three architectural problems that scale badly:

- Sequential phases (selfplay → train → eval) leave GPUs under-utilized at
  phase boundaries and make each "iteration" a 30-60 minute blocking unit.
- Binary eval gate rejects borderline-stronger candidates by random chance,
  and the rejected iterations accumulate Adam-state drift because
  `training.pt` keeps the optimizer moments across rejected runs.
- The iteration concept creates discrete data-distribution boundaries —
  stage transitions change exploration knobs and LR at the same moment, which
  the training dynamics do not absorb gracefully.

KataGo and AlphaZero avoid all three by training continuously on a growing
data pool, with snapshots exported periodically, a gatekeeper that decides
which snapshots become the new selfplay model, and (separately) a rating
tournament for monitoring. This plan ports that structure down to our scale.


## Architecture

Four independent long-running workers plus one supervisor. File-only
coordination, no IPC.

  selfplay ──(game records)──> training/selfplay/   <──(read)── train
                                                                  │
                                                                  │ (export every N steps)
                                                                  ▼
                                                           models/candidates/
                                                                  │
                                                                  ▼
                                                            gatekeeper
                                                          (200 games, 150 sims)
                                                           accept/reject
                                                         ╱              ╲
                                                        ▼                ▼
                                             models/accepted/     models/rejected/
                                                        │
                                                        │ (atomic symlink swap)
                                                        ▼
                                            selfplay polls this dir
                                                        │
                                                        │
                                                        ▼
                                            rating (optional, monitoring only)
                                            ──> ratings/elo.csv

- **selfplay driver**: Python supervisor around the existing C++
  `build/selfplay` binary. Loops in batches of N games; between batches,
  rereads `models/accepted/latest` and next invocation uses the updated
  model. Effective model-check cadence ≈ one per batch (~30-60s wall clock
  at N=300 on 2×4090). No C++ changes; no mid-batch hot-swap.
- **train (continuous)**: one torchrun DDP process. Continuous step loop,
  no epochs. Throttled by a KataGo-style train bucket that fills as new
  selfplay rows arrive and drains as training consumes samples. Exports
  ONNX at fixed step intervals into `models/candidates/`. Persists Adam
  state.
- **gatekeeper**: long-running Python process. Polls `models/candidates/`
  every 30s. Always evaluates the newest candidate against the current
  `models/accepted/latest`; any older candidates still in the directory
  are moved to `models/rejected/` unevaluated (stale-drop). Accepts iff
  candidate wins > 50% of 200 games at 150 sims (draws count as losses).
  On accept: rename into `models/accepted/` and atomically swap symlink.
  Else: rename into `models/rejected/`. Not triggered by the trainer —
  filesystem-polled, decoupled.
- **rating (optional)**: periodically plays tournament matches between
  recent accepted snapshots, writes Bayesian Elo to CSV. Pure monitoring.
  Does not gate anything.

All four run in parallel. GPU assignment per-process via
`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`; see GPU Assignment section below. Training stays DDP
across its assigned GPUs. Selfplay stays at per-device NN server threads
(preserving the existing `--nn-device-ids` flag).


## Units and cadence: step vs game

Two separate counters that move at different rates. Never confuse them.

- **1 step** = one synchronized DDP optimizer update. `batch_size` in
  this document is the **per-rank** batch (default 1024). With DDP
  across `world_size=2` GPUs, one step processes
  `batch_size × world_size = 2048` training samples total (1024 per
  rank, gradients allreduced). All bucket/replay/export math below uses
  this global 2048-sample-per-step unit. Defaults elsewhere
  (`bucket_cap_mult × batch_size × world_size`, sustained-rate formulas)
  refer to this same global quantity.
- **1 game** = one full selfplay game, ~100 moves → ~100 positions
  written to disk. Each position expands to 8 augmented rows
  (`src/mcts.cpp:725`), so ~800 rows per game on disk.

Worked defaults (2×4090, ResNet 64f/5b, 500 sims, all processes sharing GPUs):

Throughput assumption: **1.5 games/s sustained** (≈ 0.67 s/game, midpoint
of 1-2 games/s conservative range).

Important: the C++ selfplay writer stores **all 8 dihedral augmentations**
for every position (`src/mcts.cpp:725`, `augment_sample()`). One game of
~100 moves produces ~800 rows on disk, not ~100. `replay_target` in this
plan is **position-level** (matches KataGo's semantics); the bucket
internally divides by `N_AUGMENTATIONS=8` when crediting, so
`replay_target=4` means each unique position is visited ~4 times (across
different augmented views), matching KataGo.

- Row-write rate = selfplay_rate × pos_per_game × N_AUGMENTATIONS
  = 1.5 × 100 × 8 = 1200 rows/s.
- Bucket fill rate = `replay_target` × (row_write_rate / N_AUGMENTATIONS)
  = 4 × (1200 / 8) = 600 samples/s.
- Sustained training rate = 600 / (batch × world) = 600 / 2048
  ≈ **0.29 steps/s** average.
- Over 1 week: 0.29 × 604800 ≈ **180k training steps**.
- `export_every = 5000 steps` → 1 candidate per ~4.8 hours.
- Over 1 week: ~36 candidates offered. At ~50-70% accept rate: 18-25
  promotions.
- Selfplay rolls ~130k unique positions/day (1M augmented rows/day).
- Gatekeeper workload: ~36 matches × ~90s/match (shared GPU) = ~54 min
  total over a week. Negligible.

Why this matters: if we instead counted at the row-level (fill 4 × 1200
= 4800 samples/s), trainer would run at ~2.3 steps/s sustained → 1.4M
steps/week, each unique position visited 32 times. That's 8× over-replay
vs. KataGo, risks overfitting even though each visit uses a different
deterministic augmentation. Position-level replay_target with
N_AUGMENTATIONS divisor is the principled accounting.

If you prefer to reason about exports in game units (at conservative 1.5
games/s, replay=4, batch=1024, world=2):
`export_every_games ≈ export_every_steps × (batch × world) / (pos_per_game × replay_target)`.
For defaults: 5000 × 2048 / (100 × 4) ≈ 25600 games per candidate,
or ~4.7 hours at 1.5 games/s.

**Upside scenario** (throughput came in at 3 games/s sustained): 1-week
run produces ~360k steps, ~72 candidates, hits first LR milestone at day
4 and is approaching the second by end of week. Still fine, no retuning.

**Downside scenario** (throughput at 1 games/s sustained): ~120k steps
over a week, ~24 candidates, first LR milestone hit around day 7. Run
still produces a usable Elo curve; just slower. If throughput drops
below 1 games/s, consider reducing replay_target from 4 to 3 (lets
trainer do slightly more per row) or cutting gatekeeper's share.

**Future optimization**: if we move augmentation from the C++ writer
into the Python dataloader (online random aug), `N_AUGMENTATIONS`
becomes 1, pool size shrinks 8×, and `replay_target` reverts to its
direct row-level meaning. Not required for this plan; noted because it's
the cleanest long-term fix.

`selfplay_batch_games = 300` is **unrelated to export cadence**. It only
controls how often the Python wrapper re-reads `accepted/latest`. Does not
affect training step count, replay ratio, or gatekeeper verdict rate.


## Train bucket (KataGo-style replay-ratio enforcement)

Both the replay-ratio cap and the export cadence are needed. They are
orthogonal.

The train bucket is the trainer's pacing knob. Semantics:

    N_AUGMENTATIONS        = 8            # 8-fold dihedral aug at write time
    bucket                 = 0            # current credit, in samples (rows)
    max_bucket_samples     = BUCKET_CAP_MULT * batch_size * world_size
                                          # soft cap to prevent unbounded growth
    watermark_id           = 0            # max g_<id> already credited
                                          # (filename-based, not index-based)

    Scanner thread (fires every ~5s):
        # Enumerate pool, parse IDs from filenames. IDs are monotonic
        # (selfplay driver guarantees this), so new files always have
        # id > watermark_id even if older files were pruned.
        new_files = [f for f in glob("training/selfplay/g_*.bin.zst")
                     if parse_id(f) > watermark_id]
        new_rows   = sum(count_rows(f) for f in new_files)
        max_new_id = max(parse_id(f) for f in new_files) if new_files else watermark_id

        # Credit per row = replay_target / N_AUGMENTATIONS, because
        # replay_target is defined per UNIQUE position (matches KataGo).
        bucket  += (replay_target * new_rows) // N_AUGMENTATIONS
        bucket   = min(bucket, max_bucket_samples)
        watermark_id = max_new_id

    Trainer:
        while True:
            if bucket < batch_size * world_size:
                log BUDGET_SLEEP; sleep(5); continue
            batch = sample_from_window()
            loss  = train_step(batch)
            bucket -= batch_size * world_size
            step   += 1
            ...

Why filename-ID-based (not index-based): old files get pruned by the
selfplay driver once the pool exceeds `window_games`. If the watermark
were `(file_index, row_offset)` into `sorted(pool)`, pruning the
oldest N files would shift every remaining file's index down by N, so
a persisted marker at (500, X) would point to a different file after
restart. With a filename-based ID (monotonic, unique, never reused —
see Selfplay driver's crash-safe ID scheme), the watermark survives
any pruning pattern: after pruning, files with id ≤ watermark_id
simply don't exist any more, and the scanner keeps crediting only
files with id > watermark_id as before.

Persistence (at each EXPORT step, and on clean shutdown):
    save training.pt with:
        model_state_dict
        optimizer_state_dict
        step
        bucket_level            # current credit, in rows
        watermark_id            # filename ID-based watermark (int)
        rng_state               # for reproducible sampling

On resume: restore all fields. Scanner thread starts its loop from the
saved `watermark_id` — any rows that arrived during the outage are
picked up on the first scan tick and properly credited, without
double-crediting rows that were already counted. Without this, a
restart would either (a) re-credit the entire existing pool (burst of
training) or (b) start empty and BUDGET_SLEEP for hours until fresh
rows arrive. KataGo persists both `train_bucket_level` and the
last-read row index for the same reason; we use last-credited filename
ID rather than row index because our pool is pruned.

Key properties:

- `bucket` reflects **only** unconsumed selfplay credit. It drains one
  step at a time. No accumulating counters that grow indefinitely. This
  is the fix for the "loop sleeps forever at steady state" bug.
- `max_bucket_samples` caps how much training "credit" can pile up if the
  trainer is slower than selfplay for a stretch (e.g. during a TRT
  recompile). Prevents huge catch-up bursts that would briefly blow past
  the replay-ratio target. Default: `BUCKET_CAP_MULT = 64` → at batch=1024
  DDP=2, cap = 128k samples.
- At conservative fill (600 samples/s), cap is reached after ~3.6 min
  of trainer stall.
- The scanner thread reads the window (file-reference list) on a timer
  and tops up the bucket. Separate from the training step; no blocking.

Export cadence is the signal-rate knob for the gatekeeper. It controls
how often the trainer offers a new candidate for evaluation. Each export
is a cheap ONNX dump done in the training process (~1-3s blocking).


## DDP ownership of bucket and window state

`torchrun --nproc_per_node=N` launches N trainer processes. They all
share one logical training loop via collective ops, but each process
has its own Python interpreter and memory. Bucket and scanner state
must be single-sourced, otherwise ranks can disagree on whether the
current step is fundable — one rank enters `train_step` while another
is still sleeping, and the DDP allreduce hangs.

Ownership rules:

- **Rank 0 owns**: the Bucket, the Scanner thread, the file watermark,
  and the authority to decide whether this step runs. All persistence
  (training.pt checkpoint, status.json) happens on rank 0 only.
- **All ranks own**: their own WindowRingBuffer with their own ingest
  thread. Every rank scans the same pool directory and independently
  fills its ring. Random sampling inside each rank's ring uses a
  rank-offset RNG seed so batches differ — DDP then averages gradients
  as usual. Rings may drift slightly in decompression timing, but
  correctness doesn't depend on ring contents being identical across
  ranks.
- **Every step is a collective decision**:
    1. Rank 0 checks bucket; computes `step_ok: bool`.
    2. Broadcast `step_ok` from rank 0 to all ranks (single bool =
       negligible cost).
    3. If `step_ok` is False: all ranks sleep 5s and loop back.
    4. If True: all ranks run `train_step`, then rank 0 drains bucket
       by `batch_size × world_size`.
- **Resume / checkpoint**: only rank 0 writes training.pt and
  status.json. All ranks enter `torch.distributed.barrier()` around the
  save so nobody advances the step counter mid-write. On resume, rank 0
  reads training.pt, broadcasts `{step, bucket_level, watermark_id}` to
  other ranks, and all ranks load model/optimizer state identically
  from the same file.
- **Cold-start gate**: rank 0 decides, broadcasts. Same collective
  pattern.

This keeps all bucket bookkeeping single-owner (no rank-drift possible)
while still parallelizing the expensive decompression work across
ranks.


## Train loop (pseudocode)

    # Resume or fresh start — rank 0 drives; non-zero ranks wait for broadcast.
    if rank == 0:
        resume = load_checkpoint_if_exists()
    else:
        resume = None
    resume_meta = broadcast_object_from_rank0(
        resume.meta() if (rank == 0 and resume) else None)

    step = resume_meta["step"] if resume_meta else 0
    load_model_and_optimizer(model, optimizer,
                             path="training/checkpoints/training.pt",
                             exists=bool(resume_meta))

    # Rank 0 only: bucket + scanner + file watermark
    if rank == 0:
        bucket  = Bucket(max_bucket_samples=BUCKET_CAP_MULT * batch_size * world_size,
                         initial_level=resume_meta["bucket_level"] if resume_meta else 0)
        scanner = WindowScanner(replay_target=REPLAY_TARGET,
                                n_augmentations=N_AUGMENTATIONS,
                                bucket=bucket,
                                pool_dir="training/selfplay/",
                                watermark_id=resume_meta["watermark_id"] if resume_meta else 0)
        scanner.start()                       # background thread

    # All ranks: local ring buffer + ingest thread
    window = WindowRingBuffer(ring_games=RING_GAMES,
                              pool_dir="training/selfplay/",
                              rng_seed=BASE_SEED + rank)
    window.start_ingest()                     # background thread

    lr_schedule = FixedStepLR(warmup=WARMUP_STEPS,
                              base=BASE_LR,
                              milestones=LR_MILESTONES,
                              gammas=LR_GAMMAS)
    value_ramp  = LinearRamp(start=1.0, end=2.0, end_step=VALUE_RAMP_STEPS)
    score_ramp  = LinearRamp(start=0.0, end=1.0, end_step=SCORE_RAMP_STEPS)

    while True:
        # Cold-start gate: rank 0 checks window size, broadcasts.
        go = torch.tensor(0, device=device)
        if rank == 0:
            go.fill_(1 if scanner.window_games() >= MIN_WINDOW_GAMES else 0)
        dist.broadcast(go, src=0)
        if go.item() == 0:
            if rank == 0:
                log_event("COLD_START_WAIT", window_games=scanner.window_games())
            sleep(30); continue

        # Bucket gate: rank 0 checks, broadcasts.
        if rank == 0:
            go.fill_(1 if bucket.available() >= batch_size * world_size else 0)
        dist.broadcast(go, src=0)
        if go.item() == 0:
            if rank == 0:
                log_event("BUDGET_SLEEP", bucket=bucket.available(),
                                          cap=bucket.max)
            sleep(5); continue

        # All ranks: local sample + DDP step.
        batch = window.sample_batch(batch_size)
        lr    = lr_schedule(step)
        f_v   = value_ramp(step)              # value weight 1.0 → 2.0
        f_s   = score_ramp(step)              # 0 → 1 multiplier for score knobs
        wts   = head_weights_with_ramps(f_v, f_s)
        loss  = train_step(batch, lr=lr, head_weights=wts)  # DDP allreduce inside

        if rank == 0:
            bucket.drain(batch_size * world_size)
        step += 1

        # Publish ramp state at a MUCH higher cadence than export, so
        # selfplay sees a smooth ramp. Cheap JSON write; does not block
        # other ranks (rank 0 only).
        if rank == 0 and step % STATUS_PUBLISH_EVERY == 0:
            write_json_atomic("training/status.json",
                              {"step": step,
                               "score_ramp": f_s,
                               "value_ramp": f_v,
                               "lr": lr,
                               "wall_time": time.time()})

        if rank == 0 and step % LOG_EVERY == 0:
            log_metric_row(step, wall_time, samples_seen=step * batch_size * world_size,
                           lr, loss, window_positions=scanner.window_positions,
                           bucket=bucket.available(), ring_rows=window.ring_size(), ...)

        if step % EXPORT_EVERY == 0:
            # Barrier ensures all ranks have finished the step before
            # rank 0 exports/checkpoints. Prevents half-written state.
            dist.barrier()
            if rank == 0:
                path = f"models/candidates/v{step:09d}.onnx"
                export_onnx(path, atomic=True)
                save_checkpoint(step=step,
                                model=model, optimizer=optimizer,
                                bucket_level=bucket.available(),
                                watermark_id=scanner.watermark_id(),
                                rng_state=get_rng_state())
                log_event("EXPORT", step=step, path=path,
                          loss_ema=..., bucket=...)
            dist.barrier()

        if step % LR_HALVE_LOG_STEPS == 0 and lr_schedule.just_halved():
            log_event("LR_DROP", old_lr=..., new_lr=lr, step=step)

- Checkpoint (`training/checkpoints/training.pt`) includes weights,
  optimizer_state_dict, step, bucket_level, watermark_id, rng_state.
  On resume: restore all. See Train bucket section above for why the
  bucket state matters.
- Metric rows aggregated to one per 100 steps (per-step is ~100MB/day;
  aggregated is ~1MB/day). Loss EMAs and replay-ratio snapshots computed
  across the 100-step window.


## Selfplay driver (pseudocode)

    POOL = "training/selfplay"

    # Crash-safe: derive the next filename ID from what's already on
    # disk. No separate counter file to get out of sync.
    def next_id(pool_dir):
        existing = glob(f"{pool_dir}/g_*.bin.zst")
        if not existing:
            return 1
        def id_of(p):
            stem = Path(p).name[len("g_"):-len(".bin.zst")]
            return int(stem)
        return max(id_of(p) for p in existing) + 1

    # Startup cleanup: half-written .tmp files and orphan staging dirs.
    for orphan in glob(f"{POOL}/g_*.bin.zst.tmp"):
        os.remove(orphan)
    for staging in glob(f"{POOL}/staging/batch_*"):
        rmtree(staging)

    while True:
        current_model = resolve(models/accepted/latest)
        if current_model != last_logged_model:
            log_event("MODEL_SWAP", old=last_logged_model, new=current_model)
            last_logged_model = current_model

        # Read trainer's ramp state — defaults to 0 if trainer hasn't
        # published yet (very early run) or status.json is missing.
        status = read_json_safely("training/status.json",
                                  default={"score_ramp": 0.0})
        mcts_score_weight = 0.06 * status["score_ramp"]

        # Binary writes to a fresh staging dir (its own "0-indexed" game_N.bin
        # namespace); Python then renumbers and compresses into the shared pool.
        batch_id = next_batch_id()
        staging  = f"{POOL}/staging/batch_{batch_id:09d}"
        makedirs(staging)

        t0 = time.time()
        run_cpp_selfplay(model=current_model,
                         games=SELFPLAY_BATCH_GAMES,
                         output=staging,
                         mcts_score_weight=mcts_score_weight)  # passes --score-weight
        t_selfplay = time.time() - t0

        # Compute all final filenames BEFORE any rename, so that if we
        # crash mid-loop the next restart computes a new next_id that
        # is strictly greater than any already-published file.
        # (next_id is called here, AFTER all prior ranames landed.)
        start_id = next_id(POOL)
        bin_paths = sorted(glob(f"{staging}/game_*.bin"))

        positions_written = 0
        for i, bin_path in enumerate(bin_paths):
            gid   = start_id + i
            final = f"{POOL}/g_{gid:017d}.bin.zst"
            tmp   = final + ".tmp"
            with open(bin_path, "rb") as fin, \
                 zstd.open(tmp, "wb") as fout:
                fout.write(fin.read())
            os.rename(tmp, final)                 # atomic publish
            positions_written += count_rows_in_zst(final)
            os.remove(bin_path)

        # Disk pruning: after publishing, cap the pool at window_games.
        all_pool = sorted(glob(f"{POOL}/g_*.bin.zst"))
        if len(all_pool) > WINDOW_GAMES:
            for old in all_pool[:-WINDOW_GAMES]:
                try:
                    os.remove(old)
                except OSError:
                    pass  # races are benign; next iteration retries

        rmtree(staging)

        log_batch_row(batch_id, model=current_model,
                      games=SELFPLAY_BATCH_GAMES,
                      duration=time.time()-t0,
                      selfplay_duration=t_selfplay,
                      positions_written=positions_written,
                      pool_size=min(len(all_pool), WINDOW_GAMES))

Why staging + rename: the current `build/selfplay` binary (see
`src/main_selfplay.cpp:185`) starts `game_id` at 0 on every invocation
and writes directly to the final `.bin` name. A flat shared pool with
multiple invocations would either collide on names or see partial files.
The staging dir gives each invocation its own namespace; the Python
driver is responsible for (a) monotonic renaming across the whole run,
(b) zstd compression, and (c) atomic publish via `rename`. No C++
changes required.

**Crash-safety of the monotonic filename scheme**: there is no persistent
counter file. Each batch computes `start_id = max(existing IDs on
disk) + 1` atomically at the moment of first publish. If the driver
crashes mid-batch, some files from that batch may be published and some
may be in `.tmp` state. On restart:

- orphan `.tmp` files are deleted,
- staging dirs are deleted,
- the next `next_id()` call observes the max ID that actually landed
  (monotonic from the on-disk truth), and publishes the next batch
  starting at `max + 1`.

So the invariant "published filenames are strictly increasing, no ID
collisions, ever" holds across any crash pattern. The scanner watermark
(tracked by rank-0 trainer) is robust because it references specific
filenames, not counter values.

**Disk pruning**: the selfplay driver is the single writer and also
owns pruning. After each batch, if the pool has more than
`WINDOW_GAMES` files, the oldest ones (by sorted filename) are deleted.
This is why `window_games` in the knobs table actually governs disk
retention — because a process enforces it. Pruning races with the
trainer's ingest thread are benign: trainer's ingest reads file,
decompresses, copies rows into ring, closes; if a race hits an
already-decompressed file, the OS simply returns the data it already
read, and the file is removed afterwards.


## Gatekeeper (pseudocode)

    while True:
        candidates = sorted(glob("models/candidates/*.onnx"))  # lex-sort = step-sort
        if not candidates:
            sleep(GATE_POLL_INTERVAL); continue

        # Newest wins; older candidates in the queue are stale — drop them.
        newest = candidates[-1]
        for stale in candidates[:-1]:
            atomic_move(stale, f"models/rejected/{basename(stale)}")
            log_event("GATE_STALE_DROP", cand=stale)

        current = resolve(models/accepted/latest)
        # (init always seeds one, so current is never None; no first-candidate branch.)

        log_event("GATE_MATCH_START", cand=newest, baseline=current)
        t0 = time.time()
        wins, draws, losses = play_match(candidate=newest, current=current,
                                         games=GATE_GAMES, sims=GATE_SIMS)
        duration = time.time() - t0
        total    = wins + draws + losses
        score    = wins / total           # Go: count wins only; draws/losses are non-wins

        verdict  = "ACCEPT" if (total > 0 and score > GATE_THRESHOLD) else "REJECT"
        log_decision_row(newest, current, wins, draws, losses, score,
                         verdict, duration)
        log_event(f"GATE_{verdict}", cand=newest, score=score, games=GATE_GAMES)

        if verdict == "ACCEPT":
            atomic_move(newest, f"models/accepted/{basename(newest)}")
            atomic_symlink_swap(f"models/accepted/{basename(newest)}",
                                "models/accepted/latest")
        else:
            atomic_move(newest, f"models/rejected/{basename(newest)}")

`atomic_symlink_swap(target, link)` = create `link + ".tmp"` pointing to
target, then `os.rename(link + ".tmp", link)`. Guaranteed atomic on POSIX;
no window where selfplay resolves a missing or half-swapped symlink.

`atomic_move(src, dst)` = `os.rename(src, dst)` — atomic within the same
filesystem (which `models/` is).


## Rating loop (pseudocode, optional)

    # Monitoring only. Does not gate.
    while True:
        accepted = sorted(glob("models/accepted/v*.onnx"))
        to_rate = most_recent(accepted, k=RATING_POOL_SIZE)
        run_round_robin(to_rate, games_per_pair=RATING_GAMES, sims=RATING_SIMS)
        update_bayesian_elo()
        append_csv(ratings/elo.csv)
        sleep(RATING_INTERVAL)


## Learning rate schedule (fixed step schedule)

Fixed, step-based. No auto-plateau detection — train-loss EMA is too
noisy in early training and triggers premature drops.

- Linear warmup from 0 to `BASE_LR` over the first `WARMUP_STEPS`.
- Hold at `BASE_LR` through `LR_MILESTONES[0]`.
- At each milestone, multiply current LR by `LR_GAMMA` (default 0.5).
- No upper bound on total steps. Training runs indefinitely; after the
  final milestone, LR stays at the floor.

Default schedule is sized so the **first** halving reliably lands
mid-run under conservative throughput (~180k steps/week → first drop at
100k steps ≈ day 4), and later milestones only matter if throughput
outperforms or the run extends past one week. A too-early drop costs
little; a drop that never fires costs a usable LR-schedule test.


## MCTS and training-head weights (continuous = fixed + two ramps)

The phased pipeline ramps MCTS and head weights across stages. In
continuous training there are no stage boundaries, so most are fixed
throughout the run. Two step-driven ramps remain, both justified by
fresh-start instabilities the phased pipeline specifically avoids.

### Value-head weight ramp (fresh-start only)

Raw value CE drops from ~0.8 (random init) to ~0.08 (converged). If
`value_weight` is fixed at 2.0 from step 0, value loss contribution
(0.8 × 2.0 = 1.6) dwarfs policy loss and the model wastes early capacity
on a noise signal. To avoid this:

- `value_weight` ramps **linearly from 1.0 at step 0 to 2.0 at step
  VALUE_RAMP_STEPS**, then holds at 2.0.
- Ramp is step-driven, not signal-driven. VALUE_RAMP_STEPS = 30k.
  At conservative throughput (~0.3 steps/s), the ramp completes around
  28 hours in — early enough that most of the run is at the full weight.

### Score-confidence ramp (fresh-start only)

The score head (scoreMean, scoreStdev, scoreBelief) and the MCTS score
utility are untrustworthy on a random-init network: the model outputs
arbitrary point estimates, which (a) generate noisy gradients in the
score heads themselves, and (b) actively distort MCTS search by
biasing the Q values toward a meaningless score prediction. The phased
pipeline sidesteps this by explicitly holding `mcts_score_weight = 0.0`
and `head_score_mean_weight = 0.004` through bootstrap and warmup
stages (`run_loop.py:215`, `_EXPLORE[0:2]`), and even has a comment
noting that KataGo's 0.30 score weight drove our setup to divergence.

Rather than introduce stages, use a second step-driven ramp. All four
score-related knobs scale together via a single `score_ramp(step)`
factor:

    score_ramp(step) = min(step / SCORE_RAMP_STEPS, 1.0)

- `mcts_score_weight(step)`        = 0.00  + 0.06  × ramp
- `head_score_mean_weight(step)`   = 0.004 + 0.006 × ramp   (= 0.004 → 0.010)
- `head_score_stdev_weight(step)`  = 0.002 + 0.004 × ramp   (= 0.002 → 0.006)
- `head_score_belief_weight(step)` = 0.010 + 0.025 × ramp   (= 0.010 → 0.035)

SCORE_RAMP_STEPS = 50k (default). At conservative throughput, completes
around day 2 — matches the phased pipeline's "early gated / consolidate"
transition point (around iter 9-14), where the staged rationale already
said the score head is usable.

Starting floors are from the phased bootstrap values (not zero for the
training heads — a small positive weight still supplies gradient and
keeps the heads from drifting to degenerate outputs, while being quiet
enough to not poison other heads). MCTS score_weight specifically
starts at zero because its damage (biasing search) is qualitatively
different from a noisy gradient.

Ownership head is NOT ramped — per-point BCE converges quickly even
from random init, and the phased pipeline doesn't ramp it either.

### Fixed MCTS params (from late-stage `_EXPLORE` tuple)

Taken from mid-to-late stage of the current small/large presets
(`run_loop.py:215-228`), backing off the exploration aggression slightly
to avoid premature exploitation across the full run:

  mcts_c_puct           1.25      (between stage-4 1.25 and stage-5 1.1)
  mcts_dirichlet_alpha  0.15      (9×9 Go — KataGo formula: 10.83/N_legal ≈ 10/75)
  mcts_dirichlet_eps    0.22      (mid-stage value)
  mcts_temp_threshold   12        (moves with τ=1 at game start)
  mcts_win_loss_weight  1.0
  mcts_score_scale      18.0      (= 2·sqrt(81); KataGo formula)
  mcts_komi             7.5       (9×9 area scoring, half-integer → no draws)
  mcts_score_weight     0.0 → 0.06 ramp over SCORE_RAMP_STEPS  (see above)

### Fixed head weights (from `run_loop.py:419-425`, with two ramps)

  head_policy_weight        1.0
  head_value_weight         1.0   → 2.0   ramp over VALUE_RAMP_STEPS
  head_score_mean_weight    0.004 → 0.010 ramp over SCORE_RAMP_STEPS
  head_score_stdev_weight   0.002 → 0.006 ramp over SCORE_RAMP_STEPS
  head_score_belief_weight  0.010 → 0.035 ramp over SCORE_RAMP_STEPS
  head_ownership_weight     0.85   (fixed; per-point BCE stable from step 0)
  head_opp_policy_weight    0.1   (fixed; small contribution)

If experiments show a specific head is under/over-trained in continuous
mode, override individual weights via CLI — do not reintroduce stages.


## Default knobs

Sized **conservatively** for 2×4090, ResNet 64f/5b, shared-GPU
contention. Assumes ~1.5 games/s sustained selfplay → ~0.3 steps/s
sustained training → ~180k steps over 1 week. Intended run length: up
to ~1 week. Scale up only after the first run proves the pipeline.

**Training throughput**
  replay_target            4                position-level; KataGo default
                                            (internally scaled by 1/N_AUGMENTATIONS=1/8
                                            for row-bucket crediting — see Train bucket section)
  n_augmentations          8                rows written per position (src/mcts.cpp:725)
  window_games             80000            disk retention cap; selfplay driver
                                            prunes oldest beyond this (~15h at 1.5 games/s)
  ring_games               2000             per-rank in-RAM ring buffer
                                            (~1.6M rows × 6KB ≈ 10 GB per rank).
                                            This is the effective sampling window
                                            (~22 min at 1.5 games/s).
  min_window_games         2000             cold-start gate — no training until reached
  bucket_cap_mult          64               max_bucket = 64 × batch × world_size (~4 min catch-up)
  export_every             5000 steps       candidate every ~4.8h at conservative throughput
  status_publish_every     100 steps        rank 0 writes training/status.json
                                            (~5 min at conservative — smooth ramp for selfplay)
  log_every                100 steps        aggregated metric row cadence
  batch_size               1024             per DDP rank (total 2048 for 2-GPU DDP)
  value_ramp_steps         30000            value-weight ramp 1.0 → 2.0 (~28h at conservative)
  score_ramp_steps         50000            score-knobs ramp 0 → full (~46h at conservative)

**Selfplay**
  selfplay_batch_games     300              ~3-5 min per invocation at conservative throughput

**Gatekeeper**
  gate_games               200              (KataGo default)
  gate_sims                150              (KataGo default)
  gate_threshold           0.5              wins / total must exceed this (Go: draws count as losses)
  gate_poll_interval       30s              how often gatekeeper scans candidates/

**LR schedule (ResNet)**
  base_lr                  3e-4
  warmup_steps             2000
  lr_milestones            [100000, 400000, 1500000]     (steps)
  lr_gamma                 0.5               (halve at each milestone)
  # First drop @100k steps ≈ day 4 conservative / day 2 if throughput is higher.
  # Second drop @400k only reachable at ~2x conservative throughput or multi-week run.
  # Third is a guard for very long runs.

**LR schedule (ViT — override when --arch vit)**
  base_lr                  8e-4
  warmup_steps             3000
  lr_milestones            [100000, 400000, 1500000]
  lr_gamma                 0.5

**MCTS params** (fixed except mcts_score_weight; see MCTS section)
  mcts_c_puct              1.25
  mcts_dirichlet_alpha     0.15
  mcts_dirichlet_eps       0.22
  mcts_temp_threshold      12
  mcts_score_weight        0.0 → 0.06 ramp over score_ramp_steps
                                           (selfplay reads training/status.json
                                            per batch and passes --score-weight)
  mcts_win_loss_weight     1.0
  mcts_score_scale         18.0
  mcts_komi                7.5

**Head weights** (two ramps; see MCTS section)
  head_policy_weight         1.0
  head_value_weight          1.0   → 2.0   over value_ramp_steps
  head_score_mean_weight     0.004 → 0.010 over score_ramp_steps
  head_score_stdev_weight    0.002 → 0.006 over score_ramp_steps
  head_score_belief_weight   0.010 → 0.035 over score_ramp_steps
  head_ownership_weight      0.85  (fixed)
  head_opp_policy_weight     0.1   (fixed)

**Rating (optional — GPU-time-intensive, defer if uncertain)**
  rating_games             80 per pair      (~47 Elo SE; enough signal for monitoring)
  rating_sims              200
  rating_pool_size         5                most-recent accepted models
  rating_interval          7200s            (2 hrs between rounds; ~84 rounds/week)
  # At ~4 min/round shared-GPU, rating costs ~5.5 hrs/week of GPU time.
  # If trainer BUDGET_SLEEPs grow after enabling rating, extend interval
  # or disable until the run's main throughput is proven.

**GPU assignment (DEFAULT = single-GPU; override via CLI)**
  selfplay_gpus            "0"              CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES for selfplay
  train_gpus               "0"              CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES for torchrun
  gate_gpus                "0"              CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES for gatekeeper
  rate_gpus                "0"              CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES for rate
  nn_device_ids_selfplay   "0,0"            2 NN server threads on visible GPU 0
  nn_device_ids_gate       "0"
  nn_device_ids_rate       "0"


## GPU assignment

Each worker runs with its own `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`, set by the
supervisor in the subprocess environment. Inside each process, device IDs
are 0-indexed **against the visible set**, not the physical GPUs. This
preserves the existing `--nn-device-ids 0,0,1,1` convention and lets the
user slot roles onto specific physical cards.

**Default: single GPU (everything stacked on GPU 0). Override at launch.**

**2×4090 box (recommended, selfplay + training share both GPUs):**

    python scripts/run_continuous.py run \
      --selfplay-gpus 0,1 \
      --train-gpus 0,1 \
      --gate-gpus 0 \
      --rate-gpus 1 \
      --nn-device-ids-selfplay 0,0,1,1 \
      --nn-device-ids-gate 0 \
      --nn-device-ids-rate 0

    # selfplay:   4 NN server threads across both GPUs
    # training:   torchrun --nproc_per_node=2, DDP
    # gatekeeper: shares GPU 0 (brief, infrequent)
    # rate:       shares GPU 1 (infrequent)
    # CUDA driver time-slices between processes. VRAM fits all workloads
    # at our model size (model ~100MB + TRT engine ~80MB per device).

**6-GPU box (dedicated split):**

    python scripts/run_continuous.py run \
      --selfplay-gpus 0,1,2,3 \
      --train-gpus 4,5 \
      --gate-gpus 4 \
      --rate-gpus 5 \
      --nn-device-ids-selfplay 0,0,1,1,2,2,3,3 \
      --nn-device-ids-gate 0 \
      --nn-device-ids-rate 0

    # selfplay gets physical 0-3 (visible as 0-3 internally), 8 NN threads
    # training gets physical 4-5 (visible as 0-1), DDP across them
    # gatekeeper shares physical 4 (visible as 0)
    # rate uses physical 5 (visible as 0)

**Rules**

- `--<proc>-gpus` flag sets `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` for that subprocess.
- `--nn-device-ids-<proc>` is passed through to the underlying C++ binary
  and is **0-indexed against the visible set**.
- For training, `--train-gpus A,B` implies `torchrun --nproc_per_node=N`
  where N = length of the list.
- If a flag is omitted, default = single visible GPU 0.
- Overlapping assignments (e.g. gate shares a GPU with train) are allowed;
  driver handles contention.


## TRT engine cache sharing (avoid rebuild on every promotion)

Without care, every promotion triggers a 30-120s TRT engine rebuild in
the next selfplay batch — at one promotion per ~1 hour, that's a ~2-3%
throughput tax. We avoid it by making gatekeeper and selfplay share the
cache.

Requirements for the cache to hit:

1. **Single cache directory**: all three binaries (`build/selfplay`,
   `build/evaluate`, any future tool) use `models/trt_cache/`.

2. **Content-hashed cache keys**: the TRT backend must key the cached
   engine by ONNX-content-hash (or filename, since filenames are unique
   per version), **not** by absolute path. Otherwise a file that moves
   `candidates/v2000.onnx` → `accepted/v2000.onnx` invalidates its own
   cache. Implementation requirement: verify current behavior in
   `src/tensorrt_compute.cpp` (or wherever the cache key is formed) and
   fix if it uses absolute path. Prefer `{basename, content-hash,
   max_batch, device_sm}` as the key.

3. **Same dynamic-batch profile across binaries**: gatekeeper calls
   `build/evaluate` at low sims (150), selfplay calls `build/selfplay`
   at high sims (500). If each builds an engine with a different opt
   batch size, the cache misses. Fix: both binaries build dynamic-batch
   engines with the same `(min=1, max=MAX_BATCH_SIZE)` profile; only the
   opt value varies (and opt size does not invalidate the cache — TRT
   reuses the same plan file).

4. **Filename uniqueness across the run**: candidates are named
   `v{step:09d}.onnx` (monotonic, zero-padded). Acceptance is a
   `rename()` inside `models/`, so the basename is preserved. Selfplay
   resolves `accepted/latest` → `accepted/v2000.onnx`; cache key
   (basename=v2000.onnx + content hash) is identical to what the
   gatekeeper produced during the match. Hit.

With this setup, the gatekeeper match itself warms the TRT cache for the
winner. Selfplay's next batch loads the plan from disk in <1s. Rejected
candidates also leave a cached plan behind — small cost, worth keeping
for forensic/rating-rerun purposes.


## Logging and diagnostics

The loop runs autonomously. When something goes wrong you need logs
granular enough to pinpoint the misbehaving knob without re-running. All
four workers write both a human-readable text log and a structured
CSV/JSONL alongside.

**Per-process log files** (under `logs/<ts>/`):

| Process    | Text log         | Structured                     | Row cadence       |
| ---------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------ | ----------------- |
| train      | train.log        | train_metrics.csv              | one per 100 steps |
| selfplay   | selfplay.log     | selfplay_batches.csv           | one per batch     |
| gatekeeper | gatekeeper.log   | gate_decisions.csv             | one per cand      |
| rate       | rate.log         | elo.csv                        | one per round     |

**Columns in `train_metrics.csv` (aggregated per 100 steps):**
step, wall_time, samples_seen, lr, value_weight, loss_total_mean,
loss_total_p10, loss_total_p90, loss_policy, loss_value, loss_opp,
loss_length, loss_own, loss_score_mean, loss_score_stdev, loss_belief,
window_games, window_positions, bucket_samples, bucket_fill_ratio,
steps_per_sec, gpu_mem_mb

**Columns in `selfplay_batches.csv`:**
batch_id, wall_time_start, wall_time_end, model_in_use, games_played,
positions_written, duration_s

**Columns in `gate_decisions.csv`:**
wall_time, cand_step, baseline_step, games, sims, wins, draws, losses,
score, threshold, verdict, match_duration_s

**Event tags written to text logs:**

- `EXPORT step=5000 path=... loss_ema=2.3 bucket=41024`
- `MODEL_SWAP old=v000001000 new=v000002000`
- `GATE_MATCH_START cand=... baseline=...`
- `GATE_ACCEPT cand=... score=0.54 games=200`
- `GATE_REJECT cand=... score=0.41 games=200`
- `GATE_STALE_DROP cand=...`
- `LR_DROP old_lr=3e-4 new_lr=1.5e-4 step=200000`
- `BUDGET_SLEEP bucket=0 cap=131072`
- `COLD_START_WAIT window_games=412`
- `CRASH proc=... exit_code=... traceback_file=...`
- `RESTART proc=... reason=...`

**Diagnostic playbook** (symptom → which log → likely knob):

| Symptom                               | Where to look                                      | Likely knob                               |
| ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Trainer sleeping often                | train.log `BUDGET_SLEEP` frequency                 | replay_target too low, or selfplay slow   |
| Trainer bucket saturated              | train_metrics.csv bucket_fill_ratio near 1         | bucket_cap_mult too high; trainer slow    |
| Gatekeeper queue growing              | gate_decisions.csv arrival rate vs verdict time    | export_every too low or gate_games high   |
| Many GATE_STALE_DROP events           | gatekeeper.log                                     | export_every too low relative to gate rate|
| All candidates ACCEPT                 | gate_decisions.csv verdict column                  | gate_threshold too loose                  |
| All candidates REJECT                 | same                                               | gate_threshold too tight, or LR too high  |
| Selfplay using stale model            | selfplay.log MODEL_SWAP vs gatekeeper GATE_ACCEPT  | selfplay_batch_games too large            |
| LR never drops                        | train.log LR_DROP timing vs milestones             | lr_milestones set beyond run length       |
| Elo chart flat despite promotions     | ratings/elo.csv deltas small                       | gate_threshold too loose (false accepts)  |
| Elo chart jittery                     | ratings/elo.csv                                    | rating_games too small for signal         |
| TRT rebuild on every batch            | selfplay.log slow batch starts                     | cache key bug or MAX_BATCH mismatch       |

**Log retention**: keep everything for the duration of a run. Logs are
small compared to selfplay `.bin.zst` files. On `init`, old runs are
archived into `logs/archive-<ts>/`, not deleted (see Fresh-start flow).


## File layout

    training/
    ├── selfplay/                         # flat pool; no iteration subdirs
    │   ├── g_00000000000000001.bin.zst   # monotonic, sortable-by-name
    │   ├── g_00000000000000002.bin.zst
    │   └── ...                           # {epoch_ns}_{seq}_{uuid4prefix} also works
    └── checkpoints/
        └── training.pt                   # weights + optimizer_state_dict

    models/
    ├── candidates/                       # trainer writes here
    │   └── v000005000.onnx
    ├── accepted/                         # gatekeeper promotes here
    │   ├── v000000000.onnx               # bootstrap
    │   ├── v000005000.onnx
    │   └── latest -> v000005000.onnx
    ├── rejected/                         # rejected / stale-dropped candidates
    │   └── v000002500.onnx
    └── trt_cache/                        # shared between all C++ binaries

    ratings/
    └── elo.csv                           # step, model, elo, games

    logs/<ts>/
    ├── train.log
    ├── train_metrics.csv
    ├── selfplay.log
    ├── selfplay_batches.csv
    ├── gatekeeper.log
    ├── gate_decisions.csv
    ├── rate.log
    └── supervisor.log                    # run_continuous.py own log

    logs/archive-<ts>/                    # archived logs from previous init

**Game file naming**:
- Format: `g_{idx:017d}.bin.zst` where `idx` is monotonic across the
  life of a run, owned by the single selfplay driver, and computed as
  `max(existing IDs on disk) + 1` per batch (see Selfplay driver for
  crash-safety argument). 17 digits covers 10^17 games — far more than
  any conceivable run.
- For multi-writer / cluster setups (out of scope for this plan), the
  format would need a per-writer namespace such as
  `g_w{worker_id:02d}_{seq:017d}.bin.zst`, plus a coordinated pruner
  and an updated scanner that parses both fields. Designed as a
  follow-up if/when we scale beyond a single host.
- Do NOT use mtime-based ordering — fragile under clock skew / NFS.

**Atomic writes**:
- Selfplay writes each game record to `<name>.bin.zst.tmp`, then
  `os.rename(...tmp, ...bin.zst)` on success. Trainer never sees
  partial files.
- Trainer writes candidate ONNX to `v<step>.onnx.tmp` then rename.
- Gatekeeper moves candidate in/out via `os.rename` (atomic within a
  filesystem; `models/` is one filesystem).
- Symlink swap is tmp-symlink + rename (see Gatekeeper section).


## Implementation phases

All Python plus one tiny C++ change (TRT cache key if it's currently
path-based).

1. `scripts/train_continuous.py`
   - Adapt `scripts/train.py`.
   - Remove epoch loop; single continuous step loop gated by train
     bucket. Bucket maintained by a background scanner thread that tails
     `training/selfplay/*.bin.zst` (sorted by filename — monotonic).
   - Fixed step-based LR schedule with warmup + milestone halvings.
   - Two step-driven ramps: `value_weight` 1.0 → 2.0 over
     VALUE_RAMP_STEPS, and a single `score_ramp(step)` factor that
     scales `mcts_score_weight`, `head_score_mean_weight`,
     `head_score_stdev_weight`, and `head_score_belief_weight` over
     SCORE_RAMP_STEPS. Rank 0 publishes both factors to
     `training/status.json` atomically every STATUS_PUBLISH_EVERY steps
     (default: 100 steps ≈ every ~5 min at conservative throughput),
     not just at export. This gives selfplay a smooth ramp signal
     rather than multi-hour jumps tied to candidate exports.
   - Step-based ONNX export (atomic tmp + rename) into
     `models/candidates/`.
   - **Window + ring-buffer reader**: `window_games` governs disk
     retention (enforced by selfplay driver's pruner — see Selfplay
     driver). The trainer-side effective sampling window is the
     in-memory ring buffer, which is much smaller than `window_games`:
       * Each rank runs a background ingest thread that decompresses
         newly-arrived games and pushes their rows into a local ring
         buffer sized to `RING_ROWS` (default: 1.6M rows ≈ 2000 games ×
         800 aug_rows × ~6KB = ~10 GB RAM per rank).
       * When the ring is full, adding a new game's rows evicts the
         oldest equal count of rows (FIFO at row granularity; exact
         game-boundary eviction is unnecessary).
       * Each decompress is amortized: one file produces ~800 rows →
         ~400 batches at batch=2048 → ~400 steps before that file's
         rows get evicted → ingest decompresses ~1 file per 1.3s
         sustained (compatible with a few percent of one CPU core).
       * Trainer samples uniformly from its local ring. No pick-file-
         per-batch decompress cost.
     **Freshness math**: at 1.5 games/s × 800 rows/game = 1200 rows/s
     fill rate (same per rank, since each rank ingests the same files
     independently), a 1.6M-row ring evicts a row in 1.6M / 1200 ≈
     1333 s ≈ **22 minutes**. So the effective sampling window is ~22
     min of selfplay data, not hours. This is much tighter than the
     80k-file disk window, and tighter than KataGo's hour-scale shuffle
     buffer, but adequate for continuous training: the model changes
     slowly enough that 22 min of current-strength games is
     representative.

     **Replay accounting** (careful, because rings are per-rank and
     each disk row is duplicated into every rank's ring):

       Per-rank picture: one rank's ring holds 1.6M rows; that rank
       samples `batch_size = 1024` rows per step at 0.3 steps/s → 307
       samples/s from ITS ring. Per-row sample probability during the
       row's ring lifetime (per-rank) = 307 × 1333 / 1.6M ≈ **0.26**.

       Global picture: a single disk row lives in `world_size = 2` rank
       rings simultaneously (each rank decompresses the same file into
       its own ring). A training step draws `batch × world = 2048`
       samples total, spread across ranks. Expected visits per disk row
       per step = 2048 / 1.6M ≈ 0.00128. Over the row's 1333 s (≈ 400
       steps) lifetime: 0.00128 × 400 ≈ **0.51**.

       These are the same count, just framed differently: global
       visits = per-rank visits × world_size (a row appears in every
       rank's ring and gets ~0.26 draws from each). 0.51 global visits
       per disk row × 8 augmented rows per position ≈ 4 position-level
       visits per unique position, matching `replay_target = 4` with
       KataGo's semantics.

     **Duplicated ingest cost**: with `world_size = 2`, each disk file
     is decompressed twice (once per rank's ingest thread). Decompress
     throughput is ~1200 rows/s × 2 ranks = 2400 rows/s total, well
     below a single CPU core's capability. Not a concern.

     **Tradeoff**: larger ring (more RAM per rank) gives more per-batch
     diversity but doesn't change the replay accounting. Smaller ring
     (less RAM per rank) risks intra-batch correlation. 2000 games × 10
     GB/rank is a reasonable starting point for a 2×4090 / 64+ GB RAM
     box. Scale linearly with available RAM.
   - Persist checkpoint on export: model + optimizer + step +
     bucket_level + watermark_id + rng_state. Restore all on resume.
   - Writes `train.log` and `train_metrics.csv` (per-100-step rollups).
   - Respects `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` (set by supervisor).

2. `scripts/selfplay_driver.py`
   - Thin supervisor.
   - Resolves `models/accepted/latest`.
   - Invokes `build/selfplay --nn-device-ids <cfg>` into a staging dir
     (`training/selfplay/staging/batch_<id>/`); then compresses,
     monotonic-renames via `g_<idx:017d>.bin.zst.tmp` → `...bin.zst`
     (atomic) into the shared pool.
   - Crash-safe monotonic IDs: `next_id = max(existing g_*.bin.zst IDs) + 1`,
     recomputed from disk each batch. No separate counter file; no
     possibility of overwriting a published file across any crash
     pattern.
   - Reads `training/status.json` each iteration, passes
     `--score-weight = 0.06 × score_ramp` to the binary for the MCTS
     ramp.
   - **Owns disk retention**: after each batch's publish step, if the
     pool exceeds `window_games`, oldest files (by sorted filename)
     are deleted. This is the only process that deletes files from
     `training/selfplay/`.
   - Startup cleanup: deletes orphan `*.bin.zst.tmp` files and any
     leftover `staging/batch_*` dirs from a prior crash.
   - Loops on clean exit; restarted by outer supervisor on crash.
   - Writes `selfplay.log` and `selfplay_batches.csv`.

3. `scripts/gatekeeper.py`
   - Polls `models/candidates/` every `gate_poll_interval` seconds.
   - Picks the newest candidate (`sorted(...)[-1]`).
   - Moves older candidates to `rejected/` as GATE_STALE_DROP.
   - Plays match vs `accepted/latest` using `build/evaluate` at
     `gate_sims` visits and `gate_games` games.
   - On accept: atomic rename into `accepted/`, atomic symlink swap.
   - On reject: atomic rename into `rejected/`.
   - Writes `gatekeeper.log` and `gate_decisions.csv`.

4. `scripts/rate.py` (optional)
   - Watches `models/accepted/` for new promotions.
   - Periodic round-robin tournament between the k most recent accepted
     models.
   - Bayesian Elo via pairwise Bradley-Terry MLE on accumulated game
     counts.
   - Writes `rate.log` and `ratings/elo.csv`. Does not gate.

5. `scripts/run_continuous.py`
   - Top-level supervisor.
   - Parses CLI flags: `--selfplay-gpus`, `--train-gpus`, `--gate-gpus`,
     `--rate-gpus`, `--nn-device-ids-<proc>`, plus all knobs overridable.
   - Creates `logs/<ts>/` directory.
   - For each subprocess: sets `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` in env, spawns,
     tees stdout/stderr into `logs/<ts>/<proc>.log`.
   - Launches (1) under torchrun with `nproc_per_node=len(train_gpus)`.
   - Launches (2), (3), (optionally 4) as plain subprocesses.
   - Writes its own `supervisor.log` with process spawn/restart/exit
     events.
   - Handles Ctrl-C: send SIGTERM to all, wait N seconds, SIGKILL
     leftover.
   - On crash of any worker: log `CRASH`, restart after exponential
     backoff (cap at 5 min), log `RESTART`. If a single worker crashes
     3 times within 10 min, stop restarting it and alert; keep other
     workers running.
   - `init` subcommand: archives existing `training/`, `models/`,
     `ratings/`, `logs/` into timestamped `archive-<ts>/` subdirs (not
     delete); runs XQWL06 bootstrap to produce initial model; places it
     in `models/accepted/v000000000.onnx` with `latest` symlink.

6. C++ change (one file): ensure TRT cache key is content-hashed (or at
   least basename-based), not absolute-path. Verify and fix in
   `src/tensorrt_compute.cpp`. Verify MAX_BATCH_SIZE is identical across
   `build/selfplay` and `build/evaluate`. Small change; testable by
   doing a single promotion and timing selfplay startup.

7. Archive old loop: rename `run_loop.py` → `run_loop_phased.py`.
   `run_continuous.py` becomes the default entry point.


## Sanity checkpoints and scale-up decision

A 1-week run is too long to discover the pipeline is broken on day 6.
At each of these checkpoints, look at the logs and confirm forward
progress; abort and debug if signals are missing.

**6 hours in** — basic pipeline health:
- `selfplay_batches.csv` shows batches completing with
  `games_played == 300` (no crashes mid-batch).
- `train_metrics.csv` has ≥ 5000 steps logged. Loss total is decreasing
  (policy loss trending down is the strongest signal).
- At least one `EXPORT` event in `train.log`.
- `bucket_fill_ratio` fluctuates in 0.05-0.9 range (neither pinned empty
  nor pinned full).
- No repeating `CRASH`/`RESTART` events on any worker.

**24 hours in** — early strength signal:
- ≥ 5 `GATE_ACCEPT` events. If accept rate is 0% the model isn't
  improving fast enough to beat itself; check loss curves and LR.
- If accept rate is 100%, gate_threshold is too loose (every slightly-
  different weight-set wins) — not critical but tighten after the run.
- `ratings/elo.csv` (if rating enabled) shows ≥ 100 Elo gain between
  v000000000 (bootstrap) and the newest accepted. If flat, the
  gatekeeper's promotions aren't translating to real strength — check
  `gate_decisions.csv` score distribution.
- `selfplay.log` MODEL_SWAP events within minutes of GATE_ACCEPT events
  (not hours — that would mean selfplay_batch_games is too large).

**72 hours in** — steady-state check:
- Training step count roughly matches the conservative projection
  (~78k steps at 0.3 steps/s). If far below, either selfplay is slower
  than expected or BUDGET_SLEEP is pinning the trainer — check
  `bucket_fill_ratio` logs.
- Elo curve is still rising (not plateaued at ~200-300 Elo above
  bootstrap — that would suggest the model has converged for this LR
  and the next milestone hasn't hit yet).
- If LR milestone already hit, verify the drop is reflected in the
  `lr` column of `train_metrics.csv`.

**Scale-up decision (after 1-week run completes):**
- If pipeline worked and Elo is still rising at end of week: extend
  runtime, don't change knobs. Trajectory will keep improving.
- If trainer was BUDGET_SLEEPing > 50% of the time: selfplay is the
  bottleneck. Options: add GPUs to selfplay, reduce gatekeeper/rating
  GPU share, lower `replay_target` to 3.
- If bucket was saturated > 80% of the time: trainer is the bottleneck.
  Options: add GPUs to training (wider DDP), raise `replay_target` to
  5-6, or raise `batch_size` to 2048.
- If Elo gains plateau and no obvious throughput imbalance: model
  capacity is likely the limit. Scale model (64f/5b → 128f/10b) before
  scaling runtime.
- If throughput assumptions held (~0.3 steps/s sustained) and the run
  was productive: the pipeline is proven. Next run can push
  `window_games`, `export_every`, and `lr_milestones` higher with
  confidence.


## Fresh-start flow

1. `python scripts/run_continuous.py init`
   - Archives `training/`, `models/`, `ratings/`, `logs/` into
     `archive-<ts>/` subdirs. Nothing deleted.
   - Runs XQWL06 bootstrap to produce `models/accepted/v000000000.onnx`.
   - Points `models/accepted/latest` at it.

2. `python scripts/run_continuous.py run [--<proc>-gpus ...] [knob overrides]`
   - Launches selfplay driver, train_continuous (under torchrun),
     gatekeeper, and rating — all as subprocesses with GPU assignment.
   - Runs until Ctrl-C.
   - Resumable: on restart, picks up existing `training.pt` (weights,
     optimizer state, step, bucket_level, watermark_id, rng_state),
     existing selfplay records (monotonic filenames; no separate
     counter file), existing accepted models, existing logs (new run
     opens a fresh `logs/<ts>/`).


## Deviations from KataGo

Where we simplify because we are small-scale:

- Rolling window is "newest M games" (flat count) rather than KataGo's
  row-count power-law taper. Power-law matters at hundreds-of-millions
  scale; a flat window is fine at ours.
- Selfplay polls `accepted/latest` symlink from Python between C++ binary
  invocations, instead of calling `findLatestModel` in a C++ background
  thread. Simpler; avoids C++ changes; ~30-60s check cadence vs their
  20s.
- No separate shuffled-row data files (`shuffle.py`). Training samples
  directly from the flat selfplay pool. KataGo's shuffler becomes
  necessary only when the pool is too big to hold in memory; ours is
  not.
- Rating is a single-process, optional monitoring tool rather than a
  full `match` tournament infrastructure.
- Gatekeeper picks newest candidate and drops older ones (stale-drop),
  rather than FIFO-evaluating every candidate KataGo queues up. Matches
  KataGo's "throw away old candidates if you fall behind" behavior
  implemented differently.
- Go scoring: count wins only (draws count as losses), not (w +
  0.5d)/total. Draws are near-impossible under half-integer komi
  anyway.

Where we match KataGo exactly:

- Persist Adam optimizer state across restarts.
- Train bucket for replay-ratio enforcement (not monotonic-counter).
- Fixed LR step schedule (no auto-plateau).
- Fixed MCTS and head weights during continuous training.


## Open choices / tuning notes

All numbered knobs have reasonable defaults above, but these are the
most likely tuning targets based on real run behavior:

- `replay_target`: 4 (matches KataGo). If trainer BUDGET_SLEEPs
  frequently, consider 3; if trainer always saturates the bucket,
  consider 5-6. Do not go above 8 (overfitting risk).
- `export_every`: 5000 steps gives ~1 candidate per 4.8h at conservative
  throughput. Lower if you want snappier feedback; but gate_games=200
  matches cost ~60-120s each on shared GPUs, so going below 2000 starts
  queuing (and triggering GATE_STALE_DROP).
- `gate_threshold`: 0.5 is KataGo's permissive default. If Elo chart
  shows many accepts with no real Elo gain, bump to 0.52-0.55.
- `value_ramp_steps`: 30k targets ~day 1-2 completion at conservative
  throughput. If value loss plateaus before the ramp finishes, shorten
  to 15k. If still noisy at 30k, lengthen to 50k+.
- `score_ramp_steps`: 50k targets ~day 2 completion at conservative
  throughput — matches the phased pipeline's "early gated / consolidate"
  transition (iter 9-14) where the score head becomes usable. Shorten
  only if gate-match Elo gains stall specifically when MCTS starts
  using the score head (visible as a flat Elo stretch right around
  step 50k). Lengthen if the score head continues to produce obviously
  wrong point estimates past step 50k.
- `bucket_cap_mult`: 64 allows ~4 min of trainer catch-up at conservative
  fill rate. If training stalls for minutes during TRT rebuilds (cache
  bug), bucket saturates and you lose credits — fix the TRT cache first,
  don't raise the cap.
- `window_games`: 80k covers ~15h of disk retention at conservative
  selfplay rate. Shorter (e.g. 30k) gives fresher data, saves disk; not
  usually necessary. This knob controls WHEN files get deleted, not
  sampling — sampling is governed by `ring_games`.
- `ring_games`: 2000 games × 800 aug_rows ≈ 1.6M rows ≈ 10 GB RAM PER
  RANK (so 20 GB total on a 2-GPU DDP box). At 1.5 games/s fill, row
  lifetime in the ring is ~22 min — that IS the effective sampling
  window. Raise to 4000-5000 (20-25 GB per rank) for more diversity if
  you have RAM. Lower only if RAM-constrained. Tighter than KataGo's
  shuffle-buffer (which is hour-scale) but adequate for continuous
  training where model strength changes slowly.
  process.
- `lr_milestones[0]`: 100k hits day 4 at conservative throughput. If
  throughput is consistently better, first drop lands earlier — fine,
  model adapts. If consistently worse, drop never fires in a 1-week
  run — consider lowering to 80k for the next run, but raising floor
  LR (`base_lr` → smaller) is usually the cleaner fix.
- **Bigger knobs for scaling up later** (only after first run proves
  out): `batch_size` to 2048 if memory allows, `window_games` to
  200-500k for longer runs, extend LR milestones proportionally to
  expected step count.
